//! English explanations of the MTR window's traceroute options (G6.4c), sorted by key.

/// Explanations: (key, Markdown).
pub(super) const HELP: &[(&str, &str)] = &[
    ("mtr.mode", "## Mode\n\n**MTR**: continuous rounds (one probe per hop every interval) with loss and latency over time — to find where a problem starts.\n\n**Traceroute**: one pass, N probes per hop (default 3), each RTT in the Probes column and the classic `traceroute` table in the terminal (`/tool traceroute`). To record the path as it is now in a ticket."),
    ("mtr.paris", "## Paris\n\nEvery probe is the **same flow** for a per-flow load balancer (ECMP): same ports and, for ICMP, the same checksum (a payload word compensates the sequence). So all of them take one of the paths and the trace shows a real path (Augustin et al., \"Paris traceroute\", IMC 2006).\n\nOff (classic traceroute), each probe is a different flow: a hop behind ECMP shows its routers alternating, with the `ECMP n` chip — useful to **see** the load balancing. Default: on.\n\nUDP and TCP with Paris go one probe at a time (only then can the answer of a router that quotes little of the packet be told apart): a pass is slower when hops are silent."),
    ("mtr.port", "## Port\n\nUDP: the destination port (default **33434**, traceroute's; in classic mode it goes up with every probe). TCP: the service's port (default **80**; use one the firewalls on the way let through, like 443)."),
    ("mtr.probes", "## Probes\n\nHow many probes per hop in one pass (1 to 10; default 3, like `traceroute`). More probes show the variation better and, without Paris, more routers of an ECMP."),
    ("mtr.protocol", "## Protocol\n\n**icmp** (echo, default): what ping uses. **udp**: classic traceroute; the destination answers \"port unreachable\". **tcp**: a SYN to the port (like `traceroute -T`/`mtr --tcp`); gets through firewalls that drop ICMP and UDP and follows the service's path; the destination answers SYN-ACK (the connection is reset at once — the service sees a connection opened and closed) or RST.\n\nNone needs a raw socket: the ICMP errors come on the socket's error queue (`IP_RECVERR`)."),
];
